  "preamble": "Stopping of Arabic ẓāʾ ظ [ðˤ] to the pharyngealized stop [dˤ] in inherited words. In Egyptian and Levantine Arabic, where the dental fricatives merged with the dental stops, ẓāʾ is /dˤ/ in inherited words such as /dˤalma/ 'darkness' and /ʕadˤm/ 'bone', but /zˤ/ in borrowings from Literary Arabic (Wikipedia, Ẓāʾ). Egyptian 'nail' is /d(ˤ)ufr/, never */zˤufr/ (Wikipedia, Egyptian Arabic phonology, a passage tagged as needing a citation), and in most of Algeria and Morocco both ẓāʾ and ḍād are /dˤ/ (Wikipedia, Ẓāʾ). Because ḍād had already merged with ẓāʾ, the same [dˤ] stands for both letters in inherited words, and Wiktionary files Moroccan and South Levantine reflexes of ظ under spellings with ض (ضل, ضفر, ضهر, عضم). In urban Levantine speech the result can also be [zˤ] (Al-Masri 2015; Brustad & Zuniga 2019: 405–407, cited by Wikipedia).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Stopping, Fortition",
